A qualified electronic signature is validated by a process that confirms the signature is valid only if all of the following are met (E Art. 32.1):
- the certificate supporting the signature was, at the time of signing, a qualified certificate complying with Annex I (E Art. 32.1(a); E Art. 28.1);  
- the qualified certificate was issued by a qualified trust service provider and was valid at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(b));  
- the signature validation data corresponds to the data provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(c));  
- the unique set of data representing the signatory in the certificate is correctly provided to the relying party (E Art. 32.1(d));  
- the use of any pseudonym is clearly indicated to the relying party if a pseudonym was used at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(e));  
- the electronic signature was created by a qualified electronic signature creation device (E Art. 32.1(f); E Art. 30.1);  
- the integrity of the signed data has not been compromised (E Art. 32.1(g)); and  
- the requirements of Article 26 were met at the time of signing (E Art. 32.1(h)). Article 26(26) requires that an advanced electronic signature be uniquely linked to and capable of identifying the signatory, be created using signature-creation data the signatory can use under his sole control, and be linked to the signed data so any subsequent change is detectable (E Art. 26.26).

Operational and service requirements for validation:
- the validation system must provide the relying party the correct result and allow detection of any security‑relevant issues (E Art. 32.2).  
- the Commission may specify reference standards for validation by implementing acts; compliance with Article 32(1) is presumed where validation meets those standards (E Art. 32.3).  
- a qualified validation service may be offered only by a qualified trust service provider that performs validation in compliance with Article 32(1) and allows relying parties to receive the validation result in an automated, reliable and efficient manner bearing the provider’s advanced electronic signature or seal (E Art. 33.1).

- A qualified trust service provider must verify the identity (and any specific attributes) of the natural person by appropriate means and in accordance with national law; the provider may verify directly or rely on a third party (E Art. 24).

- Acceptable verification methods are (E Art. 24):
  - physical presence of the natural person (E Art. 24);
  - remote verification using electronic identification means, provided that prior to issuance a physical presence was ensured and those electronic identification means meet the assurance levels "substantial" or "high" set out in Article 8 (E Art. 24; E Art. 8);
  - by means of a certificate of a qualified electronic signature or of a qualified electronic seal issued in compliance with the physical‑presence or remote methods above (E Art. 24);
  - other identification methods recognised at national level that provide equivalent assurance to physical presence (equivalence to be confirmed by a conformity assessment body) (E Art. 24).

Electronic signatures qualify as advanced electronic signatures when they meet all of the following requirements (E Art. 26):
- (a) they are uniquely linked to the signatory;  
- (b) they are capable of identifying the signatory;  
- (c) they are created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can, with a high level of confidence, use under his sole control; and  
- (d) they are linked to the data signed therewith in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable. (E Art. 26)

- Selection: The controller must use only processors who provide "sufficient guarantees" to implement appropriate technical and organisational measures so that processing meets the Regulation's requirements and protects data subjects' rights (Art. 28.1; Rec. 81).

- Formal appointment: Processing by a processor must be governed by a contract or other legal act under Union or Member State law, binding the processor to the controller and setting out the subject‑matter and duration of the processing, the nature and purpose of the processing, the type of personal data and categories of data subjects, and the obligations and rights of the controller (Art. 28.3; Rec. 81).

- Mandatory contractual stipulations: The contract or legal act must in particular require that the processor:
  - processes personal data only on documented instructions from the controller (including for transfers), unless required by law (Art. 28.3(a));
  - ensure persons authorised to process data are bound to confidentiality (Art. 28.3(b));
  - take all measures required by Article 32 (security) (Art. 28.3(c));
  - assist the controller in responding to data‑subject rights and in meeting obligations under Articles 32–36, as appropriate (Art. 28.3(e)–(f));
  - at the controller's choice, delete or return all personal data after the end of the provision of services, and delete existing copies unless law requires storage (Art. 28.3(g)); and
  - make available to the controller all information necessary to demonstrate compliance and to contribute to audits, and immediately inform the controller if an instruction infringes the Regulation (Art. 28.3(h)) (Art. 28.3).

- Sub‑processors: If a processor engages another processor, the same data protection obligations must be imposed on the sub‑processor (Art. 28.4).

- Operational constraint: The processor and any person acting under the authority of the controller or processor who has access to personal data shall not process those data except on instructions from the controller, unless required to do so by Union or Member State law (Art. 29.1; Art. 32.4).

Elements that should be included in a code of conduct:

- Specific topics and rules specifying the application of the Regulation, in particular with regard to:
  - fair and transparent processing;
  - legitimate interests in specific contexts;
  - the collection of personal data;
  - pseudonymisation of personal data;
  - information provided to the public and to data subjects;
  - the exercise of data‑subject rights;
  - information/protection of children and parental consent;
  - measures and procedures referred to in Articles 24 and 25 and security measures referred to in Article 32;
  - notification and communication of personal data breaches;
  - transfers of personal data to third countries or international organisations;
  - out‑of‑court proceedings and other dispute‑resolution procedures for disputes between controllers and data subjects.
  (G Art. 40.2)

- Provision for out‑of‑court dispute resolution and other dispute procedures (G Art. 40.2(k)).

- Consultation of relevant stakeholders, including data subjects where feasible, when drawing up, amending or extending the code (G Rec. 99).

- Calibration of obligations to sector‑specific characteristics and the needs of micro, small and medium enterprises, taking account of the risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons (G Rec. 98).

- Guidance on implementation of appropriate measures and on demonstration of compliance (e.g. risk identification, assessment and best practices to mitigate risk) may be provided by codes of conduct (G Rec. 77).

- Where compliance is monitored by a body in connection with the code, that body should demonstrate independence and expertise, and have procedures to assess eligibility of controllers/processors, to monitor compliance, to handle complaints and to avoid conflicts of interest (G Art. 41.2).

- The Regulation covers non‑contractual obligations as an autonomous concept and expressly covers non‑contractual obligations arising out of strict liability (RII Rec. 11).

- It also applies to non‑contractual obligations that are likely to arise (RII Art. 2).

- However, the following non‑contractual obligations are excluded from its scope (RII Art. 1):
  - (a) non‑contractual obligations arising out of family relationships and relationships deemed by the law applicable to such relationships to have comparable effects including maintenance obligations;
  - (b) non‑contractual obligations arising out of matrimonial property regimes, property regimes of relationships deemed by the law applicable to such relationships to have comparable effects to marriage, and wills and succession;
  - (c) non‑contractual obligations arising under bills of exchange, cheques and promissory notes and other negotiable instruments to the extent that the obligations arise out of their negotiable character;
  - (d) non‑contractual obligations arising out of the law of companies and other bodies corporate or unincorporated regarding matters such as creation, legal capacity, internal organisation or winding‑up, and personal liability of officers/members or auditors in statutory audits;
  - (e) non‑contractual obligations arising out of the relations between settlors, trustees and beneficiaries of a trust created voluntarily;
  - (f) non‑contractual obligations arising out of nuclear damage;
  - (g) non‑contractual obligations arising out of violations of privacy and rights relating to personality, including defamation (RII Art. 1).

- The provided text does not state a standalone or explicitly named "right to explanation." (G Rec. 39)

- The Regulation does, however, grant data subjects rights to transparency and information about processing, including:
  - a right of access to personal data and to be informed of the purposes, recipients, and the logic involved in any automatic personal data processing, and, at least when based on profiling, the consequences of such processing (G Rec. 63);
  - an obligation on the controller to inform the data subject of the existence of profiling and the consequences of such profiling (G Rec. 60);
  - general transparency duties and the right to obtain confirmation and communication of personal data being processed (G Rec. 39);
  - modalities to facilitate exercise of those rights (access, rectification, erasure) and an obligation to respond without undue delay and at the latest within one month (G Rec. 59).

- Conclusion: The context supplied does not describe a separate "right to explanation" term, but it does require that controllers provide information about the logic and consequences of automated processing and profiling and to facilitate access to relevant information (G Rec. 63; G Rec. 60; G Rec. 39; G Rec. 59).
